+## Follow-up: corrective boots after uncertain introduction history
+
+The frozen histories contain point-level uncertainty about older introducing
+livepatch inventory or boot/source identity. Node401 also has ambiguous
+activation/transition points for modules 2 and 6. Those points remain unknown;
+all eleven exact introduction rules and every canonical upstream release bound
+remain unchanged. The correction does not infer the first introduction or
+pretend that the old .95 release proves absence of the backported defect.
+
+Each of the twelve applicable nodes has a later retained corrective boot after
+its last affected or unknown point, followed only by classifiable fixed states
+through the current observation. All global freshness, required-period baseline,
+coverage/reconstruction and attestation checks still pass. Eleven nodes' first
+qualifying final boot is into 6.12.113 on October 18 or 19; Node401 first boots
+corrected 6.18.49 at `2026-10-18T01:32:54Z`, before its later 6.12.113 boot. The
+per-node result uses the actual retained exact transition, rather than forcing
+every node's date to its latest .113 observation. The 6.18 correction is already
+part of this dossier's canonical stable rules.
+
+The evaluator now permits that fully safe terminal interval to establish
+mitigation while retaining the earlier exposure uncertainty in its reason. It
+requires an actual corrected-kernel boot after the last affected or unknown
+point; a fixed current sample, reported-release change or interface/configuration
+change alone does not suffice. Any later unknown or affected point blocks the
+interval unless followed by another qualifying corrective boot. Supported exact
+effective times are used only for exact boot evidence; otherwise retained
+observation bounds are preserved. This is a supported final mitigation bound,
+not a claim about the globally earliest possible mitigation or the exact first
+security impact. The storage role remains outside this tenant perf assessment.
+
+All evaluations are regenerated by the normal tool against the unchanged
+`61904e...` snapshot. The existing submitted-draft recovery baselines are retained
+byte for byte until separately authorized guarded synchronization. No production
+collection, hand-edited result, fabricated historical tuple or new API field is
+used.
