 that the Node entered production later. A synthetic current-kernel row or an
 unreviewed repository pin cannot establish an earlier state.
 
+After those freshness, coverage, baseline and attestation checks pass, an
+uncertain historical point can still precede a supported final mitigation
+interval. A retained boot into an upstream-corrected kernel after the last
+affected or unknown point establishes mitigation only when every subsequent
+point, including the current observation, is classifiable as safe. A fixed
+current observation or reported-release change alone is insufficient. Later
+uncertainty or reintroduction blocks that interval until another qualifying
+corrective boot. The result preserves the earlier uncertainty in its reason and
+uses the boot's supported exact effective time or retained observation bounds;
+it does not assert the earliest possible mitigation or earlier non-exposure.
+An inferred boot time is not substituted for exact timing, and a boot without
+defensible transition bounds cannot resolve the uncertainty. All-known histories
+retain their existing first-mitigation and atomic livepatch-replacement rules.
+
 Each dossier declares the global upstream introduction and fix, stable-branch
 version rules, Node roles, kernel options, and sysctls relevant to reachability.
 Branch rules also cover vulnerable backports older than the mainline
